<p>Was asked to take a look at this, consider this my crit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But I'm still not totally sure on how writing a tale actually works. Does it need to be on an existing SCP? Can they be completely standalone?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, tales can be completely stand-alone as long as they, in some way, concern the Foundationverse in some way or suggest so. Tales and GOI formats make up a large part of the site and if you're unfamiliar with the format, I highly recommend looking into them. The <a href="/top-rated-tales">Top Rated Tales</a> is probably going to be a help with that, as well as just being around in the community for a little bit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Do you have any suggestions that could make it more unique?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd like to reaffirm <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607740234" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;">cybersqyd</a></span>'s point about human time loops but also posit the query of why the future-Foundation doesn't have access to the present-day SCP file about the spacecraft, if time is so circular and all. You know, surely someone in the present-day would have written it down somewhere that they do an abiogenesis at the particular date that the space-craft takes off.</p>
<p>Having the story be a tale would probably fix this, but you could also exploit that for a more unique twist on the idea. What if future-Foundation goes through the wormhole with the intent to cause abiogenesis but one of the crewmates accidentally kills the specimens in-transit? What happens then? Lots of story potential there, as well as character-work.</p>

