<p>I'm dead on 400 words, so I couldn't touch on this in the original post. The future Foundation does have access to the present-day SCP files. The personnel that eventually go on to travel through the wormhole (that are alive in the present day) are trained in anticipation for the wormholes materialisation. The specific details, however, aren't told to the crew. The Foundation can't tell them that they will ultimately die on this mission, nor the ultimate purpose of the mission. Doing so would alter their behaviour and therefore could mess something up.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if future-Foundation goes through the wormhole with the intent to cause abiogenesis but one of the crewmates accidentally kills the specimens in-transit?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The problem is, the mediation of abiogenesis was accidental. The simple act of landing on the Earth in the past seeded it with life through contamination of DNA present on the spacesuits of the landing team. Of course, the Foundation, from the recovered logs of the spacecraft, would know about the accidental seeding of life. They very fact it was accidental in the recovered logs means the Foundation would keep the crew going into the wormhole in the dark, as filling them in on all the details could jeopardise the mission and lead to humanity never existing in the first place.</p>
<p>Doing it this way would kind of ruin the mid-point twist. Until the mid-point, the crew (and the reader) aren't meant to know the planet is Earth. As far as the crew is concerned, they're going to assess the habitability of a potential new planet humanity could colonise. The O5s know this isn't really the case (on account of the logs from the ancient spacecraft), but they can't tell the crew this as once again, it could change the crews behaviour and lead to ramifications in the present.</p>
<hr />
<p>I like the idea though, and think doing it this way could work if I changed things around a bit. Maybe the O5s knows the planet is Earth from the past, but don't tell the crew that. When the specimens are destroyed, one of the crew has to land on the primordial Earth and remove their spacesuit (which at this point in Earth's history would kill them) to seed the planet manually through the contamination of the bacteria, nucleases, DNA etc. present on and within their body? Could even have them do it in a tide pool, which is where abiogenesis is theorised to have occurred.</p>
<p>Do you think that would fix the idea?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

